For an inline element containing a block-level box, Ladybird reports
the inline’s pieces and each block’s own border box, in content order,
with zero-size rects for empty pieces before the first block and after
the last. All three engines give the same three rects for an inline
holding one block. For several consecutive blocks, Gecko and WebKit also
report one rect per block: Gecko’s nsLayoutUtils::GetAllInFlowBoxes()
replaces each MozBlockInsideInlineWrapper frame with its principal
children in AddBoxesForFrame(), and BoxToRect::AddBox() reads their
border boxes, while WebKit’s LineBoxBuilder::constructBlockContent()
makes one spanning inline box per block. But Blink’s LayoutInline::
AddChildAsBlockInInline() groups consecutive blocks in one anonymous
LayoutBlockFlow, so LayoutInline::QuadsForSelfInternal() returns one
middle rect for each group of blocks.

The widths differ too: Gecko and Ladybird use the block’s own border
box, while Blink and WebKit use the available width of the line.

With the block in the bounding client rect, scrolling the inline into
view now centers the whole block, which matches what Blink gets from
LayoutObject::AbsoluteBoundingBoxRectForScrollIntoView(). Gecko’s
PresShell::ScrollFrameIntoView() follows only GetNextContinuation()
and leaves out the block, while WebKit centers the rect from its
RenderElement::absoluteAnchorRectWithScrollMargin(), which spans from
the top of the block to the bottom of the inline’s last text.

Ladybird reports each empty piece at the start of its anonymous line,
before the block’s top margin or after its bottom margin. That’s
where Blink reports them too.
